      "eyebrow": "CCTV and the law",
      "heading": "Signs, retention and UK GDPR",
      "html": "<p>Under UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018, the operator needs a lawful reason and must be transparent, including clear signs. The Commission says the law sets no fixed retention period: the purpose decides how long footage is kept, and it should be deleted when no longer needed. The Information Commission publishes video surveillance guidance. In England and Wales the Surveillance Camera Code of Practice (Protection of Freedoms Act 2012, section 33) must be had regard to by relevant authorities such as local authorities and police. It is not a general duty on private operators.</p><p>On your walk, check: is the sign there and readable? Is footage kept for the stated time, and no longer? Quickler keeps the sign photo and the retention setting. Whether the use is lawful is for your data protection officer. Check the latest Information Commission guidance.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Access control",
      "heading": "Doors, readers and failure",
      "html": "<p>Test that a valid credential opens the door and an invalid one does not. Then check what happens when power fails. An escape-route door is usually designed to fail safe. A secure-area door may fail secure. A wrong choice is a fire safety problem too.</p><p>Quickler records each door, its test and its fail state, with a photo. It does not sign off the design. The installer and the responsible person do.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Alarms",
      "heading": "Detection and response",
      "html": "<p>Check that zones cover what they should, tamper protection works, and a triggered alarm reaches its target: a monitoring centre, a keyholder or on-site staff. An alarm that reaches nobody is a bell on a wall.</p><p>Quickler keeps your zone checks, tamper tests and response path, with photos and times. It does not monitor the alarm.</p>"
    },
