      "eyebrow": "Sources",
      "heading": "Official sources",
      "html": "<p>The Information Commission's CCTV guidance says UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018 set no fixed minimum or maximum retention period for surveillance footage: the purpose of the processing decides, and footage should not be kept longer just because the system's default allows it. Its transparency checklist asks for clearly visible, readable signs that say the system is in use, who runs it, why, and who to contact. The Commission notes that its CCTV guidance is under review because of the Data (Use and Access) Act, so check for the latest version. The Surveillance Camera Code of Practice comes from section 33 of the Protection of Freedoms Act 2012 (England and Wales): a relevant authority, such as a local authority, the Greater London Authority, a police and crime commissioner or a chief officer of police, must have regard to it. Failing to follow it does not by itself create liability. Fire safety design for escape doors was not checked here, so ask the responsible person or the installer. Official sources checked on 18 November 2026.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/cctv-and-video-surveillance/guidance-on-video-surveillance-including-cctv/how-can-we-comply-with-the-data-protection-principles-when-using-surveillance-systems/\" rel=\"noopener\">CCTV and video surveillance: complying with the data protection principles (Information Commission)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/advice-for-small-organisations/cctv-and-dashcams/cctv-for-your-organisation-things-you-need-to-do/\" rel=\"noopener\">CCTV for your organisation: things you need to do (Information Commission)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2012/9/section/33\" rel=\"noopener\">Protection of Freedoms Act 2012, section 33: effect of the code (legislation.gov.uk)</a></li></ul>"
    },
